From: Sebastian Matkovich <sebastian.matkovich@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ufshci: bRefClkFreq only when it differs, and not fatal

The F(x)tec Pro1X's UFS device answers the write of bRefClkFreq with a
failed query response (seen with 0043 on 2026-11-01: a QUERY RESPONSE
UPIU in the right place, response code 0xfd/0xff), and the controller was
given up: no da0, no root pool. Read it first and write it only when it
differs, and go on when the device refuses, as Linux does
(ufshcd_set_dev_ref_clk). Its boot loader set the clock long ago.

---
diff --git a/sys/dev/ufshci/ufshci_dev.c b/sys/dev/ufshci/ufshci_dev.c
index dd196b1..d5f1b9f 100644
--- a/sys/dev/ufshci/ufshci_dev.c
+++ b/sys/dev/ufshci/ufshci_dev.c
@@ -252,16 +252,25 @@ ufshci_dev_reset(struct ufshci_controller *ctrlr)
 int
 ufshci_dev_init_reference_clock(struct ufshci_controller *ctrlr)
 {
-	int error;
+	uint64_t value = UINT64_MAX;
 	uint8_t index, selector;
 
 	index = 0;    /* bRefClkFreq is device type attribute */
 	selector = 0; /* bRefClkFreq is device type attribute */
 
-	error = ufshci_dev_write_attribute(ctrlr, UFSHCI_ATTR_B_REF_CLK_FREQ,
-	    index, selector, ctrlr->ref_clk);
-	if (error)
-		return (error);
+	/*
+	 * As Linux does: write bRefClkFreq only when it differs, and go on
+	 * when the device refuses. The F(x)tec Pro1X's UFS device answered
+	 * the write with a failed query response (2026-11-01), and the whole
+	 * controller was given up; its boot loader set the value long ago.
+	 */
+	if (ufshci_dev_read_attribute(ctrlr, UFSHCI_ATTR_B_REF_CLK_FREQ,
+	    index, selector, &value) == 0 && value == ctrlr->ref_clk)
+		return (0);
+	if (ufshci_dev_write_attribute(ctrlr, UFSHCI_ATTR_B_REF_CLK_FREQ,
+	    index, selector, ctrlr->ref_clk) != 0)
+		ufshci_printf(ctrlr, "bRefClkFreq stays %#jx (not %#x)\n",
+		    (uintmax_t)value, ctrlr->ref_clk);
 
 	return (0);
 }
